/*!
    \title Qt for HarmonyOS Window Settings Example
    \example platform/harmonywindowsettings
    \examplecategory {Mobile}
    \meta tag {widgets,harmonyos,window}

    \brief Changes platform settings of the windows of a Qt application on
    HarmonyOS.

    Each window of the example changes settings of its own platform window,
    the \c window.Window that the window is shown in. All windows can keep
    the screen on and hide their content in screenshots and screen
    recordings. Sub windows can also have rounded corners and be resized by
    dragging their edges.

    The settings are changed by methods of \c window.Window:
    \c setWindowKeepScreenOn(), \c setWindowPrivacyMode(),
    \c setWindowCornerRadius() and \c enableDrag(). The corner radius is
    measured in vp. A window gets its \c window.Window from
    QNativeInterface::QHarmonyWindow on the Qt thread, as a
    QHarmonyJsObjectRef handle. Each control hands the handle and the new
    value to the JS thread:

    \snippet platform/harmonywindowsettings/main.cpp 0

    On the JS thread, the task dereferences the handle and calls the method.
    Each of the methods returns a promise. The handlers attached to the
    promise log the outcome in the window. They post the text to the Qt
    thread with QNativeInterface::QHarmonyApplication::runOnQtThread(). An
    error that the call throws is logged in the same way:

    \snippet platform/harmonywindowsettings/main.cpp 1

    \c setWindowPrivacyMode() requires the
    \c {ohos.permission.PRIVACY_WINDOW} permission. The example declares it
    in its \c CMakeLists.txt with \l {qt_add_harmonyos_permission()}:

    \snippet platform/harmonywindowsettings/CMakeLists.txt 0

    The controls show the current settings of the platform window.
    \c getWindowProperties() and \c getWindowCornerRadius() return them
    synchronously, so the example reads them with
    QNativeInterface::QHarmonyApplication::runOnJsThreadAndWait(), which
    returns the result of the task:

    \snippet platform/harmonywindowsettings/main.cpp 2

    The example reads the settings each time the window is shown, once it
    is exposed, as the platform window is shown by then. The platform does
    not report whether a window can be resized by dragging its edges, so
    the example applies the initial state of that check box instead:

    \snippet platform/harmonywindowsettings/main.cpp 3

    \section1 Running the Example

    Press \uicontrol {New sub window} to open a sub window. Change its
    corner radius, or clear \uicontrol {Resizable by dragging its edges} and
    try to resize the sub window by dragging an edge.

    To check \uicontrol {Keep the screen on}, set a short screen timeout in
    the system settings. While the option is set and the window is shown,
    the screen stays on.

    To check \uicontrol {Hide the content in screenshots and recordings},
    set the option and take a screenshot. The content of the window does not
    appear in it.

    The corner radius and resizing by dragging depend on the device. On a
    device that does not support them, the calls fail and the window logs
    the error.
*/
